('2022-244ME01', 'Mémoire de Master', NULL, '244ME01', '#051707'),
('2022-24N01', 'Principles of neurobiology', 'This course covers the principles of the central nervous system organization, the mechanisms involved in the development of cortical circuits and functional specificities of the major anatomical regions of the brain.', '24N01', '#7D7DD7'),
('2022-24N02', 'Introducing the human brain: from structure to function', 'This course focuses on the functional neuroanatomy of the human brain. It will cover the fundamentals of regional organization with functional considerations and will illustrate the region\'s cardinal manifestations in neurological and psychiatric diseases. The course is intented for non-medical students preparing a master degree in the domain of neuroscience. The maximum number of students will be 30. The course format will be a combination of lectures with pratical demonstrations utilizing postmortem human brain preparations, MRI and neurofunctional images.\n\nThis course will require active participation and presence. Failing to attend classes (without good reasons) leads to failure of the exam.\n<b>\nSince the number of participants is limited, students should register to ancilla.stefani@unige.ch IN ADDITION to their faculty or doctoral school.\n</b>\nDescriptive \n1) Gross-anatomy of the human brain I \n2) Gross-anatomy of the human brain II \n3) Microscopic structure of the human brain \n4) Spinal cord and the peripheral nervous system (neuroanatomy of pain, spinal cord injuries) \n5) Brain stem and cerebellum (vital functions, neuroanatomy of the subcortical activation system, brain stem injuries, ataxie)\n6) Diencephalon and basal ganglia (thalamic injuries, diseases of the extrapyramidal system)\n7) Cerebral cortex I. Parietal, insular and lateral occipito-temporal cortex (representation of body and space, object recognition, \'where \' and \'what\' system, neglect, agnosie)\n8) Cerebral cortex II. Medio-basal cortex and the limbic system concept  (emotion, memory, homeostasis, hormones, stress,  default network, amnesia)\n9) Cerebral cortex III. The frontal and superior temporal cortex (executive functions, hearing, language, aphasia)   \n10) Human brain development and evolution.\n\nCourse and exam in English', '24N02', '#BC67AB'),
('2022-24N03', 'Introduction to cognitive and affective neuroscience', 'This course covers the important aspects of cognitive neuroscience, from perception to high levels functions. It presents the concepts and the interdisciplinary techniques used to study the cerebral substrates of cognitive functions and behavior in humans. All sessions will illustrate the value and the convergence of different methodological approaches such as neuropsychology, brain imaging, electrophysiology, psychophysics and computer modeling as well as techniques used for animals (for example single cells recordings).\n\nMoodle: https://moodle.unige.ch/course/view.php?id=4132\nTo access moodle, please contact delphine.jochaut@unige.ch', '24N03', '#F24E9A'),
('2022-24N04', 'PET and MRI Imaging in Neuroscience', 'The objective of this course is to acquire theoretical and practical knowledge in the field of MRI and PET imaging in neuroscience. This course will present the main applications of these techniques, each time with a theoretical presentation of basic concepts for experimental data analyses. This module is divided into several classes: 1. the basic principles of PET neuroreceptor imaging, from the production of radioisotopes to the clinical and pre-clinical applications. Particular emphasis will be given to the study of dopaminergic neurotransmission system in schizophrenia and the advances made by PET imaging in understanding this disease. 2. PET analysis and quantification. This course will detail the main steps involved in obtaining metabolic images. After an introduction on the foundations of compartmental modeling, an example of cerebral neurotransmission will be shown. 3. PET activation study and introduction to SPM. This course will provide the methodological basis for the analysis of PET activation data. An example of activation study will be detailed and the participants will become familiar with SPM in the practical lessons. 4. Morphometric MRI study with SPM. This course will present the methodology voxel-based morphometry(VBM). The practical exercise will help to familiarize with SPM and techniques of spatial normalization and segmentation. 5. Analysis of fMRI by SPM. This module will provide the basic methodology for the analysis of fMRI data. Simple examples are given and the participants will become familiar with SPM in the pratical lessons. 6. Small animal imaging: MicroPET and b-Microprobe. This course will introduce the development of PET imaging in vivo small animal. A visit to the different platforms: the cyclotron, MRI and PET will be held at the end of the module.', '24N04', '#0EB085'),
('2022-24N05', 'Techniques for investigating Brain Functions', 'This course provides an overview of methods currently used to investigate brain functions, including not only neuroimaging techniques (structural, metabolic, and/or functional), but also clinical approaches and behavioral tests psychophysical, electrical or magnetic stimulation, and animal models. The course provide the basic knowledge to follow more advanced courses in cognitive neuroscience and practical modules in advanced brain imaging training.', '24N05', '#AFFD69'),
('2022-24N07', 'Neurobiology of vigilance states', 'The module will address the following themes: investigation and regulation of vigilance states; neuroanatomical, neurochemical and electrophysiological basis; molecular basis of vigilance states; regulation and molecular basis of circadian rhythms; autonomous and endocrine functions during sleep; physiopathology of vigilance states; brain activation, mnesic activation and sleep; functions of sleep.', '24N07', '#E5D75F'),
('2022-24N08', 'Psychiatric Neuroscience', 'This courses presents clinical cases and related emerging issues. It discusses the fundamental basis of brain functions in a clinical oriented perspective. The main areas of psychiatry will be covered from different angles in order to provide students with the necessary information to understand the physiological processes responsible for brain function. Due to the interaction of social , psychological and biological determinants, the unique position of psychiatry may be better studied by using the detailed knowledge of these three components combined.', '24N08', '#42D44D'),
('2022-24N10', 'Clinical and Experimental Neuropsychology', 'The interpretation, assessment, diagnosis and prognosis value of signs and the treatment of the main syndromes of clinic neuropsychology, as aphasia, apraxia, visual agnosia, hemi-neglect, dementia  will be discussed in several sessions. These disorders will be illustrated by video of clinical case or examples of clinical production.\n\nMoodle: https://moodle.unige.ch/course/view.php?id=6533\nTo access moodle, please contact delphine.jochaut@unige.ch', '24N10', '#7D3BCF'),
('2022-24N12', 'Introduction to applied data science (I2ADS)', 'Course description\nThe course I2ADS, \"Introduction to Applied Data Science\" (3 ECTS for Master, 2 ECTS for PhD students), is aimed at students of all levels with a strong focus on computer science to train them in integrating the techniques that form the pillars of open & reproducible science.  This module does not replace any Unige computational course on any of the topic it features, and is meant instead as an overview of how the different computing pieces introduced fit together. As such, lectures on a given topic addressed during the course can be studied more in depth after the course to strive for mastery of that topic. This course is independent of the course EDSAN (Example of Data Science Applications to Neuroimaging), and can be attended by students from any background. The details of the course content are indicated in the \'Detailed Curriculum\' section below.\r\n\nRegistration to this course has to be carried out at this link: https://tinyurl.com/i2ads2022\n\nDetailed Curriculum\nSchedule (all lectures are 2 hour-long on Monday morning, except the first lecture which is on Tuesday afternoon because of \'Jeune federal\')\n\nLecture	Date & Time\r\nLinux Part 1	TUE SEP 20, 2PM-4PM\r\nNote: the first course is on a Tuesday afternoon instead of Monday morning due to the \"Jeune federal\"\r\nLinux Part 2	MON SEP 26, 09AM-11PM\r\nLinux Part 3	MON OCT 03 , 09AM-11PM\r\nGIT Part 1	MON OCT 10 , 09AM-11PM\r\nGIT Part 2	MON OCT 17, 09AM-11PM\r\nGIT Part 3	MON OCT 24 , 09AM-11PM\r\nGIT Part 4	MON OCT 31 , 09AM-11PM\r\nNote: this lecture will be recorded if I cannot find a replacement instructor as I will not be on site\r\nPython Part 1	MON NOV 07 , 09AM-11PM\r\nPython Part 2	MON NOV 14 , 09AM-11PM\r\nPython Part 3	MON NOV 21 , 09AM-11PM\r\nPython Part 4	MON NOV 28 , 09AM-11PM\r\nIntro to Machine Learning Part 1	MON DEC 05 , 09AM-11PM\r\nIntro to Machine Learning Part 2	MON DEC 12 , 09AM-11PM\r\nIntro to Machine Learning Part 3	MON DEC 19 , 09AM-11PM\r\n\r\n', '24N12', '#F04B3C'),
('2022-24N13', 'Trends in Computational Neuroscience', 'This \'Trends in Computational Neuroscience\' is an interdisciplinary course that will explore the application of computational methods in understanding the structure and function of the brain. It will cover a wide range of topics, including deep learning in neuroscience, generalized linear models for spike-train modeling, modeling neural dynamics with dynamical systems, model fitting and selection, reinforcement learning, and Bayesian decision-making.\r\nLectures will consist of both theoretical presentations and practical, hands-on applications using Python to analyze simulated and real data. Students will have the opportunity to put their knowledge into practice through mini-projects and will be graded based on their performance in these projects. This course is designed for students with any background who are interested in understanding how computational methods can be applied to study the brain.\r\n', '24N13', '#9259CA'),
('2022-24N14', 'Open and reproducible science  MODULE EDSAN, \"Examples of Data Science Applications in Neuroimaging\"', 'Open and reproducible science is an approach strongly emphasized to address the current reproducibility crisis revealed by the difficulty of reproducing published scientific experiments from fields ranging from machine learning to medicine. Initiatives have been conducted at all geographical scales to tackle this issue present globally: the European Commission published in December 2020 a report \"Reproducibility of scientific results in the EU\", the head of strategy of the Swiss National Science Foundation (SNSF) -- Katrin Milzow -- recently declared that \"Open Science is a strategic priority in [their] multiannual program 2021-2024\" and the University of Geneva established in 2020 a roadmap until 2023 for Open Science. In this roadmap, brought forward this year (2021) by a general communication from the rector of University of Geneva, is mentioned the specific objective of training on open science tools and practices. The two modules proposed below on data science and neuroimaging aim at precisely addressing this objective.\nModule EDSAN, \"Examples of Data Science Applications in Neuroimaging\", illustrates the use of data science in neuroimaging which is a highly inter-disciplinary field pushing forward general computational tools:  the well-known machine learning library scikit-learn in Python was developed largely by neuroimaging scientists, and so were Jupyter Notebooks and the most popular cloud solution to run them, Binder. As such, this module would benefit both students versed and not versed in neuroimaging and bioinformatics, as they would both discover applications in a highly dynamic field at the forefront of computational technologies, and learn how to collaborate within teams of varied expertise as they are typically found in research labs and the industry. This module depends on knowledge of Module IORDS, but previous attendance to Module IORDS is not required.\n To join the course, please register here: https://tinyurl.com/edsan2022', '24N14', '#A84D16'),
('2022-24N15', 'Projet de recherche : présence laboratoire + travaux semestriels', NULL, '24N15', '#A1AA48'),
('2022-24N151A', 'Projet de recherche en neurosciences: présence laboratoire + rapport semestre 1', NULL, '24N151A', '#C077C0'),
('2022-24N151B', 'Projet de recherche en neurosciences: seminars attendance year 1', NULL, '24N151B', '#362D96'),
('2022-24N152A', 'Projet de recherche en neurosciences: présence laboratoire + rapport semestre 2', NULL, '24N152A', '#DC349E'),
('2022-24N152B', 'Projet de recherche en neurosciences: seminars attendance year 2', NULL, '24N152B', '#35EB06'),
('2022-24N153A', 'Projet de recherche en neurosciences: présence laboratoire + rapport semestre 3', NULL, '24N153A', '#E7A201'),
('2022-24N16', 'Statistical and computational neuroscience', 'This course is an introduction to modeling of multiscale neural dynamics using statistics and dynamical systems. The two first classes will be devoted to refreshers on linear algebra, Python, and statistics. The two main topics will be (1) how to analyse and fit statistical models on spike trains data, (2) how to model and analyse neuronal current dynamics and local field potentials. The main tools introduced for this are generalized linear model, neuronal, neural mass, and neural fields models. Lectures will include both presentations and Python-based applications to simulated and real data. Example datasets include neuronal spike train, local field potential, intracranial EEG.', '24N16', '#11681E'),
('2022-24N17', 'Scientific 3D applications', 'This course aims at providing the fundamentals skillset required to develop scientific 3D applications (e.g. 3D simulations, VR/video game based experimental designs). You will learn by practicing (coding in C# programming language and using the Unity3D game engine) following a step-by-step approach to let you become as autonomous as possible with 3D programming knowledge and technics. \rThis course is open to people interested in scientific 3D applications. Priority will be given to Master and PhD students, but Post-Docs and other academic positions are also encouraged to attend this course. \rThis course is limited to a maximum of 12 participants.', '24N17', '#78EF97'),
('2022-24N18', 'Introduction to applied statistics for health data analysis', 'Session 1	-	Definitions\r\n-	Descriptive analyses and graphs	15 Feb\r\nSession 2	-	Estimation, p-values\r\n-	statistical inference	8 March\r\nSession 3	-	Principle of statistical inference\r\n-	T test, chi-square test	15 March\r\nSession 4	-	Non-parametric tests (eg Mann-Whitney)\r\n-	Tests for paired data	22 March\r\nSession 5	-	Statistical power\r\n-	Sample size calculation	29 March\r\nSession 6	-	Linear regression\r\n-	Interpretation and application	26 April\r\nSession 7	-	Scientific writing of statistics\r\n-	Exam (1 hour)	3 May\r\n', '24N18', '#B66B52'),
('2022-24NMEM', 'Mémoire écrit Projet de recherche', NULL, '24NMEM', '#315123'),
('2022-24NSOUT', 'Soutenance  projet de recherche', NULL, '24NSOUT', '#6010E6'),
