nancial assets based on\nthe stochastic discount factor (SDF) methodology. This methodology gives the representation of the price\nof any nancial asset as the expectation of its stochastically discounted nal payo. It allows to treat in\na unied way the pricing problem of nancial assets under general market conditions, including settings\nwith, for instance, stochastic interest rates and volatilities. We rst introduce the SDF approach to the\nrepresentation and the computation of asset prices in a discrete-time setting. Applications to the pricing\nproblem under stochastic volatilities or stochastic interest rates will be also highlighted. We then address\nthe empirical analysis of SDF models, by showing how it can be naturally developed within the Generalized\nMethod of Moments (GMM) setting. Examples of real data applications to asset pricing problems, relevant\nboth for academic research and in the practice, will be discussed.', 'S413055', '#886759'),
('2022-S413056', 'Financial Econometrics', 'The course will develop the econometrics tools and models in the context of financial markets in static and dynamic environment. \nThe first part will covers static models and tools such as CAPM, APT, Value at Risk, Expected Shortfall  and scoring procedure, while the second part will focus on dynamic analysis build around ARMA models, GARCH models and Stochastic volatility models. Thorough the course, statistical tools such as linear regression, asymptotic theory, non-parametric estimation and bootstrap procedure  will be discussed in detail. Illustration will be discusses in class. The seminar will implement those methods within the framework of MATLAB\n\nThe seminar is part of the course and mandatory.', 'S413056', '#58EDAF'),
('2022-S413057', 'Financial Economics', '1) Course contents:\n\nThe course first deals with decisions making in risky situations and how to measure risk and risk aversion. It then introduces basic concepts of financial economics and financial asset pricing theory\nsuch as preferences & utility, portfolio selection, Capital Asset Pricing Model, Arrow-Debreu Pricing, the Consumption Capital Asset Pricing Model (CCAPM), Static arbitrage, and the Arbitrage pricing theory (APT). It specifically deals with distinction between the equilibrium and the arbitrage perspectives on valuation and pricing and how both conditions imply the existence of stochastic discount factors such that the price of any security is simply its discounted expected payoff.\n\n2) Teaching approach:\n\nThe course is based on lectures and exercises. The exercise sessions are compulsory and are part of the course. Course notes will be provided before each session via moodle.', 'S413057', '#28A670'),
('2022-S413058', 'Mémoire de maîtrise', NULL, 'S413058', '#A3F8FC'),
('2022-S413059', 'Learning to Teach Finance', 'In this course, you will find yourself performing a variety of roles, which include tutoring, facilitating, marking students work, and acting as an intermediary. The exact tasks will be defined by the course instructor responsible for the undergraduate course.\r\nA. Admissible tasks: To be admissible, a task should not be financially rewarded and should be structured and organized at the University of Geneva though it could take place outside the university. It should correspond to one of the following three categories:\r\n1. Tutoring and teaching assistantship: running exercise sessions and tutorials, marking assignments/essays/project presentation, elaborating education material/ case studies/webinar/ MOOC, external teacher coordination.\r\n2. Event organization: Active participation in organizing important events including conferences, workshops, seminars.\r\n3. Research assistantship: research support, data warehousing.\r\nB. Eligible Students\r\nEligible students for this course should have\r\n\' 15 ECTS successfully completed with an average grade of at least 4.5 \r\n\' Approval of the course instructor with whom he/she will be working as Teaching Assistant\r\nSelection of candidates will be based on academic excellence and motivation. The application file is composed of an exhaustive list of courses (completed with grades and not completed) at undergraduate and graduate levels in finance, economics, statistics, econometrics, probability, and mathematics, as well as a letter of motivation. The application should be submitted to the course coordinator by the fixed deadline for the corresponding semester.', 'S413059', '#DECB61'),
('2022-S413060', 'Advanced Investments', 'The course is an introduction to macroeconomic issues and policies in open economies. After presenting the main concepts related to national accounts, the first part deals with the implications of financial market integration, focusing on current account and global imbalances, the accumulation of foreign reserves, asset trade and portfolio diversification. The second part starts from equilibrium pricing in international financial markets, covered and uncovered interest parity conditions, and then reviews models of exchange rate determination in the short- and the long-run, their empirical validity and implications for monetary policy. It also addresses important topics in international finance, such as: the choice of the exchange-rate regime; the determinants of international capital flows and sovereign yield spreads; currency crises; financial contagion; current account sustainability and sovereign debt crises. \nEach class will consist partly of lectures covering the five first chapters and partly of class room discussions of academic articles devoted to the selected topics in chapter 6.\nThe lectures will be based on teaching notes and on recommended readings from academic journals and textbooks. There will be exercise sessions as well. \nActive class participation will be encouraged and will thus imply that required readings be done in advance. We will also devote some time to discuss relevant research papers as well as ideas for Master thesis research papers in the field of investments.', 'S413060', '#8D58F7'),
('2022-S413062', 'Internship thesis / Mémoire de stage', NULL, 'S413062', '#70CC2B'),
('2022-S413063', 'CFA General Research Challenge', 'a) Analysis of a Public Company: Teams research a publicly traded Swiss company. The Swiss CFA Society, in collaboration with the university, carefully selects the company which is the same for all teams from Switzerland. Students have the opportunity to meet with company management and participate in an analyst conference call. Company management presents to the teams and participates in a Q&A session.\n\nb) Mentoring By a Professional Research Analyst: Each team works with a Swiss investment professional, who mentors the team on research process and reviews and critiques its report. The Swiss CFA Society, in collaboration with the university, assigns an investment professional to each team.\n\nc) Topical Presentations By Investment Professionals: A seminar series is organized around the competition in which investment professionals from different firms give in-class presentations on various topics related to the competition. Presentation topics range from structuring and writing of professional analyst reports, career opportunities in investment analysis, and effective communication and presentation skills to current policy debates. Active student participation is requested.\n\nd) Writing a Research Report: Each team produces an initiation of coverage report on the publicly traded Swiss company. The report is reviewed and scored by a group of judges at the end of the semester.\n\ne) Presentation of Research to a High-Profile Panel of Experts: Teams present their research to a panel of experts from top financial institutions at the end of the semester.\n\ne) Advancement to the CFA Institute Global Challenge: The team with the highest combined report and presentation score is the winner. The winners of local competitions advance to compete against teams within their geographic region, culminating in a global finale competition.', 'S413063', '#10AE16'),
('2022-S413067', 'CFA Advanced Research Challenge', '(Re)Writing a Research Report:\nEach team produces an initiation of coverage report on its chosen company. In this course students substantially revise and improve the report submitted during the course CFA General Research Challenge (5.1). The report is reviewed and scored by a group of judges.\nPresentation of Research to a High-Profile Panel of Experts:\nTeams present their research to a panel of experts from top financial institutions. The team with the highest combined report and presentation score is the winner.\nAdvancement to the CFA Institute Global Challenge:\nThe winners of local competitions advance to compete against teams within their geographic region, culminating in a global finale competition.', 'S413067', '#C7BE63'),
('2022-S413068', 'Global Asset Management', 'The course deals with the theoretical foundations of international investments, empirical evidence, and applications in a real-world setting. Globalization is a major trend affecting the asset management industry. We will then discuss international diversification of portfolios and the problem of global asset allocation, international asset pricing, hedging currency risk, factors that generate returns, ETFs, Crypto-currencies, and global performance evaluation.', 'S413068', '#287D7B'),
('2022-S413071', 'Wealth Management and Law in Practice', NULL, 'S413071', '#0FC943'),
('2022-S413080', 'Impact Investing', 'Impact investing is a rapidly growing industry with the potential to catalyze much needed private capital to address development issues in commercially viable ways. This course will provide students with fundamental tools from finance and development economics to critically assess the progress and challenges of the industry. It will focus on impact investing initiatives in emerging markets of high relevance for achieving the Sustainable Development Goals. The course will expose students both to the nascent academic literature in the field and to practical knowledge developed by the industry. It will capitalize on the unique role of Geneva and Switzerland as a hub for impact investing by inviting practitioners to share their experience with students, and to provide real practical problems for students to address in their final projects. The course is sponsored by the Geneva Finance Research Institute (GFRI), as an expression of GFRI\'s mandate and commitment to teaching and research on Finance and Society. The course is targeted at students of the Master in Wealth Management (GEMWeM) and is open also to other interested master students, with a basic background in economics or finance.', 'S413080', '#3C2772'),
('2022-S413167', 'Portfolio Management', '\r\nPart I - Tony Berrada : This section of the course starts by reviewing the standard modern portfolio theory with a particular focus on estimation and implementation issues. We then discuss the main performance measures used in the portfolio management industry and assess their relative benefits. Particular attention is given to statistical pitfalls and implementation issues associated to these measures. We then move on to dynamic asset allocation looking first at the Merton problem and the dynamic programing approach. Then, we consider the martingale approach and the equivalent static problem. \r\nEquipped with these methodologies we look at a number of topics particularly relevant in today\'s fund management industry including the core-satellite approach, risk parity, risk budgeting and the use of derivatives in asset allocation.\r\n\r\nPart II - Ines Chaieb : The second part of the course deals with the theoretical foundations of international investments, empirical evidence, and applications in a real world setting. Globalization is a major trend affecting the asset management industry. We will then discuss international diversification of portfolios and the problem of global asset allocation, international asset pricing, hedging currency risk, factors that generate returns, and global performance evaluation.', 'S413167', '#FCD033'),
('2022-S413170', 'Hedge Funds', 'Utilizing the most recent academic research, students investigate its relation to industry practices by analyzing a HF trading strategy using collected data in their research project. Students are immersion into recent academic trends; particularily HFs as liquidity providers, HF replication strategies, Chinese HFs markets and the impact of HFs on financial market stability. Also, insights from an industry professional(s) help students translate/constrast this knowledge with the real world of HF investing.\r\n\r\nEach class consists of a lecture combined with classroom discussions centered in particular on recent academic article(s) . These articles will present important HF topics which can serve as the basis for the short research project/paper. In this project, students will be expected to use databases and apply simple empirical analysis techniques in order to analyse HFs\' strategies and to develop a meaningful interpretation of their performance. Occasionally, a small part of class time will be devoted to discuss progress on the required short research paper (see below), as well as potential topics for Master theses.\r\nActive class participation will be strongly encouraged, based on the required readings and class discussions.  \r\n\r\nCourse Outline:\r\nChapter 1 - An Introduction to Hedge Funds\r\nChapter 2 - Understanding Hedge Fund strategies\r\nChapter 3 - Identifying (priced) Hedge Fund risk factors  and asset pricing models\r\nChapter 4 - Analyzing the performance of Hedge Funds \r\nChapter 5 - Sources and limits of hedge fund performance\r\nChapter 6 - Replicating hedge funds returns with liquid asset classes\r\nChapter 7 - Analyzing hedge funds flows\r\nChapter 8 - Do hedge funds destabilize financial markets?\r\nChapter 9 - An overview of the Chinese Hedge Funds market\r\nChapter 10 - Invited guest speaker', 'S413170', '#E9DB75'),
('2022-S417002', 'Commodity Price Mechanisms', 'This course provides an overview of commodity markets, including commonly trading commodity contracts; commodity pricing mechanisms (exchange & OTC); and commodity pricing and trading concepts.  It then examines in detail the economics of forward pricing in commodity markets.  The emphasis is on structural models of commodity pricing.  I analyze both storable and non-storable commodities, and show the strengths and weaknesses of structural (fundamentals-based) models.  We will also discuss how manipulation--non-competitive pricing--can influence forward curves.   Renewables/environmental products pricing is also covered.  The course format is lecture/discussion.', 'S417002', '#D2CE3F'),
('2022-S417003', 'Energy I (Oil & Gas)', 'The course covers essential technical characteristics of these commodities, quality differentiation and requirements, price discovery and benchmarks, and various contracts for physical or financial delivery. It takes place over two successive week-ends (Friday afternoon and Saturday morning, for a total of 12 hours per week). It features several outside speakers in addition to lectures delivered by the two professors in charge. It is evaluated on the basis of a take-home exam consisting in writing a short essay to address one of several questions proposed.\nThe course is open only to students enrolled in the Master of Science in Commodity Trading.', 'S417003', '#9DBAE7'),
('2022-S417005', 'Metals, Ores & Minerals', 'Introduction to mineral economics\n\nKey metals and their minerals; Exploration, production and supply; Demand by major sector\n\n\nIron ore to steel\n\nSupply of iron ore - countries, producers, exporters; Steel manufacturing and key steel producers (countries, companies); Demand for steel - industries, consumers, importers; Trade flows for iron ore and steel products; Pricing of iron ore and steel products\n\n\nCoking coal\nKey physical characteristics: coking vs. steam coal; Coal supply/demand \' key producers/exporters and consumers/importers; Pricing\n\nBauxite to aluminium\n\nSupply of bauxite and alumina - producers/exporters; Aluminium smelting and key producers (countries, companies); Demand for aluminium - industries, consumers, importers; Trade flows for bauxite and aluminium; Pricing of aluminium\n\n\nCopper ore to copper\n\nSupply of copper ore - producers/exporters; Copper smelting and refining and key producers (countries, companies); Demand for copper - industries, consumers, importers; Trade flows for copper ores, concentrates and finished copper; Pricing of copper\n\n\nMinor metals and minerals\n\nBase metals: zinc, lead, tin, nickel; Concentrates; Pricing and TC/RC calculation; Rare earths\n	Precious metals\n\nGold supply and demand fundamentals; prices and relation with interest rates; Silver supply and demand fundamentals; prices; PGM (platinum group metals) supply and demand fundamentals for platinum and palladium\n\n\n\n	Trading and hedging metals on the London Metal Exchange\n\nThe origins of the LME; LME participants and contract characteristics; Forward price quotations; Arbitrage example; Hedging examples (long, short, with options); Forward price quotations; Arbitrage example; Hedging examples (long, short, with options); Forward curves for key base metals; Relationship between stocks and prices; Price volatility; Swaps (iron ore); Other metal exchanges.', 'S417005', '#0DE261'),
('2022-S417009', 'Hedging Tools & Techniques', 'The course focuses on financial derivatives instruments and the markets in which they are exchanged. The entire workflow is analyzed: how deals are initiated, managed and closed. The motivations for derivatives trading are explained and it is clarified how risk transformation takes place.  \n\nThe course is based on slides presented by the teacher and the guest speaker(s). The slides do not constitute a textbook, they are a guide to facilitate lectures. Consequently, students are requested to actively take part during the course and be prepared to take their own notes, which will be crucial to prepare for the final exam.  \n\nAs a prerequisite to successfully attend the course, students should have a good knowledge of physical commodity markets and spot transactions taking places. \n\nThe course is related to ethics and responsibility to the extent that the most important exchange-traded derivatives instruments trading regulations are introduced and discussed.', 'S417009', '#8D1B4A'),
('2022-S417015', 'Shipping Market Risk & Risk Management', 'Introduction to the freight market\nIntroduction to freight derivatives\nForward freight agreements (FFA)\nFFA clearing and margins\nTechnical analysis & freight trading\nShip price risk management\nModelling freight rate volatility\nRisk management using option I\nRisk management using option II\nValue at risk of shipping freight rates\nReal options in shipping', 'S417015', '#C65A9B'),
('2022-S417019', 'Trade Finance Banking Instruments', 'The flow of a typical trade finance transaction\nMethods of payment\nUCP 600\nTransport documents\nLetters of indemnity\nTransferable, back-to-back, revolving, \'green/red clause\' documentary credit\nDeed of assignment\nSuretyship guarantees (bonds / \'cautions\')\nBid & performance bonds\nPayment guarantees and stand-by letters of credit', 'S417019', '#6A542D'),
('2022-S417022', 'International Economics and Trade', 'This course is divided in two parts. The first part provides an overview of the tools used in international economics to understand and analyze the determinants and impact of international transactions at both the microeconomic (international trade) and macroeconomic (international finance) level. The second part of the course provides a deep dive into the world of international commodity trading. The first part of the course is taught by Professor Olarreaga and the second part of the course by Professor Ostensson. \r\n\r\n', 'S417022', '#E2004C'),
('2022-S417024', 'Business Ethics and Human Rights', '\r\nThe academic discipline of business ethics deals with a broad range of issues that present operational and reputational risks to individuals, corporations, and industries. Being able to handle ethical issues professionally is a success factor for your career and your company\'s performance. It is also important for the standing of the industry in which you are involved.\r\n\r\nThis module of business ethics presents ethical theories as a tool to reflect on standard practices in the workplace and for specific social and environmental risks in business operations in the commodity trading industry. Case illustrations and guest speakers will focus on typical business and human rights challenges in the commodity trading industry.\r\n\r\nSocial Responsibility\r\nVirtue ethic/Deontology/Utilitarianism\r\nEthics in Trading\r\nCommodity Trading and the Media', 'S417024', '#4AD614'),
('2022-S417025', 'Master Thesis', NULL, 'S417025', '#FFBE4C'),
('2022-S417029', 'Traineeship', NULL, 'S417029', '#7BA756'),
('2022-S417030', 'Quantitative Methods', 'The course aims to provide students with data-based applications of the basic techniques for probabilistic modelling and inference commonly applied in the field of finance in order to analyse data, choose between investments and control market risk. \n\nIn the first part of the course we review the fundamentals of statistical inference and estimation, with a focus on linear models. In the second part we introduce the students to basic inferential problems encountered in the time-series analysis of financial data and to their statistical peculiarities. We also introduce applications of the Maximum-Likelihood Estimator to estimate dynamic volatility models that are useful to assess market risk. The last part presents a stochastic structural model of commodity prices and introduces the students to reduced form ARMA modelling and the Box-Jenkins procedure\n', 'S417030', '#119F1E'),
('2022-S417031', 'Types of Financing for Commodity Companies', 'This module presents the various financing methods that are available to the commodity and\nshipping market players.\nThe module first deals with the types of financing that are typically used by or available to\ncommodity traders and their partners such as:\n\' Transactional trade finance ;\n\' Inventory Financing - Repo finance ;\n\' Borrowing base ;\n\' Prefinance and Pre-export finance, which are two different types of financing yet\nhaving some common features;\n\' Payment Risk Assumption (\'PRA\') & Credit Risk Insurance (\'CRI\'), two credit risk\nmitigating tools that share common aspects but also significantly differ.\nThe module then covers other types of financing that can be useful to commodity traders\nwhen they reach a certain stage of development, integrate some aspects of the value chain\n(e.g. upstream, midstream, downstream, assets,\') and/or are used by other commodity\nmarket participants (E&P pure players, mine owners, shipping entities,..)\n\' Reserve Base Lending (\'RBL\')\n\' Project Finance (\'PF\')\n\' Asset Finance, Acquisition finance\nBridge Loan (before Bond issue, IPO or refinancing)\n\' Revolving Credit Facility (\'RCF\')\n\nA very interactive course divided into phases:\n1) Lively presentations during which participants actively listen and take notes in order to\nacquire the \'theory\', however based on illustrations, examples and real situations,\n2) Q&A sessions, discussions about peripheral, yet eminently related subjects, where\nparticipants are encouraged to actively participate in order to illustrate and supplement the\nprevious presentation, and\n3) Case studies, exercises and/or readings to ascertain that the content of each section is\nsuccessfully assimilated.\n4) Regular consolidation during which the participants are given the opportunity to relate\nwhat they have gathered in order to make sure most important concepts are acquired.', 'S417031', '#0CDAB0'),
('2022-S417032', 'Information Technologies and Innovations', 'Innovation in the IT world : how and if we can  anticipated the future, how to boost innovation in the enterprise\nLatest technologies and their use : mobile technologies, internet liberalization, IoT and their applications, big data, security in IT\nForthcoming and potential interesting technologies : quantum computing, quantum cryptography, autonomous entities, artificial intelligence\nIssues on security and privacy\nThe GAFA and their impact in the business world and the daily life of citizens and enterprises.\nBusiness considerations with the use of IT', 'S417032', '#BF1ED9'),
('2022-S417034', 'Commodity Price Mechanisms', 'This course provides an overview of commodity markets, including commonly trading commodity contracts; commodity pricing mechanisms (exchange & OTC); and commodity pricing and trading concepts.  It then examines in detail the economics of forward pricing in commodity markets.  The emphasis is on structural models of commodity pricing.  I analyze both storable and non-storable commodities, and show the strengths and weaknesses of structural (fundamentals-based) models.  We will also discuss how manipulation--non-competitive pricing--can influence forward curves. Renewables/environmental products pricing is also covered. The course format is lecture/discussion.', 'S417034', '#21084B'),
('2022-S417041', 'Legal Aspects & Regulations', 'The intention of this module is to give you an understanding of the principles of law involved in international trade. You will meet legal issues in other parts of the course, particularly in relation to shipping, insurance and financial instruments. This module will focus on the law governing the actual sale transaction. Because English law is the law of choice for the majority of international trade transactions, we will focus on English legal provisions. \nThe module will cover the following areas:\n\' Basic English contract law and the English Sale of Goods Act 1979\n\' Legal provisions relating to sale contract terms, e.g. delivery, transfer of risk & title, incoterms, price, quality & time, nomination, safe ports, laytime & demurrage, assignment & novation, and documentation & termination.\n\' Legal relationships between the different actors in the trade transaction.\n\' Rights, duties and remedies of sellers and buyers.\n\' Exclusion clauses and force majeure.\n\' Letters of Indemnity (LOIs).\nWhilst ethics and sustainability are not specifically taught as part of this module, you will be encouraged to appreciate that understanding the operation of the law in assessing your own contractual rights in case of dispute can assist in de-escalation where parties encounter non-contractual counterparty behaviour.\n', 'S417041', '#45DEED'),
('2022-S417042', 'Business Ethics and Human Rights', 'The academic discipline of business ethics deals with a broad range of issues that present operational and reputational risks to individuals, corporations, and industries. Being able to handle ethical issues professionally is a success factor for your career and your company\'s performance. It is also important for the standing of the industry in which you are involved.\nThis module of business ethics presents ethical theories as a tool to reflect on standard practices in the workplace and for specific social and environmental risks in business operations in the commodity trading industry. Case illustrations and guest speakers will focus on typical business and human rights challenges in the commodity trading industry.', 'S417042', '#B1CF61'),
('2022-S417046', 'Special Course', '2 x 6 crédits ECTS de cours exceptionnels pour les étudiants qui travaillent dans une société en Suisse et liée au négoce. L\'étudiant doit faire la demande auprès du Comité Scientifique qui validera ou non ces crédits : Acquis/non acquis / 2 x 6 ECTS of exceptional courses for students working in Switzerland in a company linked to Commodity Trading. The student has to make the request to the Scientific Committee which will valide or not these credits : Achieved/non achieved', 'S417046', '#D32283'),
('2022-S417052', 'Financial Statements Analysis', 'The course examines the basic principles of and approaches to assess the risk of the firm. Based on exercises and real examples, it lays the groundwork for linking the information contain within the financial statements and the credit risk that capital providers may estimate. Throughout the course, students will gain appreciation of several ratios that measure the liquidity and solvency risks of a company. An introduction to performance ratios will be carryout also. Lastly, the links between credit risk and performance is developed.', 'S417052', '#D8C506'),
('2022-S417053', 'Information Technologies and Innovations', 'The commodity trading industry is facing a significant transformation driven by a surge of volatility in commodity prices, intensification of regulations and compliance requirements, proliferation of complex products and transactional models, reduction of margins and a willingness to act in larger parts of the supply chain. These external challenges have re-shaped the strategy and the vision of many of the corporations operating today in the commodities industry.   \n\nIn this context, the role of information technologies has become of extreme relevance in underpinning both the design of these strategies and their implementation; either contributing to the reduction of costs and operational risks through common data and information architectures, straight through processing and real time reporting or by supporting new revenue generation business models through continuous innovation.\n\nThis course explores information technologies from the perspective of a commodities trading corporation. It will provide a number of exemplars of the application of information technologies and the latest innovations in the commodities industry. Moreover, it will analyze the importance of IT and business alignment to achieve above mentioned objectives and how to enable, in practical terms, a strategy focused information technology organization.\n\nInformation will be presented in the form of use cases, academic research, articles and whitepapers published by respected scholars and experts in the field, and the actual disclosures of major multi-national companies in the industry, including service providers, consultancy companies, software houses...', 'S417053', '#3F348D'),
('2022-S417054', 'Shipping', 'Module Outline\n1- Introduction to world trade & shipping markets\nInternational commodity trade & seaborne transportation, International shipping fleet, Units of measurements, Main shipping sectors, Types of ships, ownership and operations\n2- Shipping Freight Market and Freight Rate Formation\nTypes of charter parties, Economics of the freight market, Supply and demand and freight rate determination, Time charter rate formation, The Market for Ships, and Shipping Cycles\n3- Ship Chartering I - Worldscale calculation, voyage estimation and chartering process,\nTanker voyage rates (Worldscale), Chartering process, negotiation and freight calculation, Voyage cash flow analysis, Dry bulk voyage estimation, Tanker voyage estimation\n4- Ship Chartering II \' Charter parties, post fixture operations, Laytime calculations\nCharter parties & documentation, Charter party forms , Post fixture operations, Bill of Lading and other documents, Laytime calculation\n5- Liner & Container Shipping\nLiner shipping history, liner operations, the conference system, Liner tariffs and pricing mechanism, Containerisation, Intermodalism, global alliances\n6- LPG, LNG and Chemical Tanker Shipping\nWhat are LPG and LNG cargoes? LPG and LNG tanker fleets, demand and supply. Freight and prices in the liquefied gas shipping markets. Chemical products and trade. Chemical tankers fleet and descriptions. \n7- Risk and Risk Management in Shipping \nIntroduction to Baltic Freight Indices, Freight risk assessment, freight rate risk management using FFAs and Options. Examples of tanker, dry bulk, gas and container freight hedging using FFAs. \n8- Port & Terminals Operation \nSeaport and terminals, different types of ports, measuring port productivity and throughput, congestion and decongestion, port services to shipping, and port costs and revenues.\nReasons for Storage of Commodities, Storage costs and expenses, oil and oil product storage, metal storage and LME operations, Agri product storage.', 'S417054', '#B95EF9'),
('2022-S417055', 'Soft Commodities', 'Agri-commodities market: Grains and oilseeds, and softs such as coffee, sugar, cotton, cocoa (EK)\n1) What are the products, supplies?\ni) Prices: Grains and Oil Seeds: corn, soybeans, wheat, soybean meal, soybean oil, rough rice, canola, oats Softs: coffee, sugar, cocoa, cotton, orange juice, lumber Meats and Dairy: hogs, cattle, feeder cattle, poultry, dairy Aquaculture: fish\nii) Production areas: US and World Weather: Crop Explorer, Growing Seasons, and Stages of Development Soils Supply and Demand Factors Supply and Demand Price Impacts Grain Price Impact on Livestock Monthly news report on grains International Grains Council\nUN State of the World: agriculture, fisheries, forests UN State of Commodity Markets USDA Ag Projections to 2023 World Bank Commodity Prospects and Outlook FAO STATS, Food Outlook and Ag Outlook to 2023 Agricultural Market Information System (AMIS) Market Monitor\n2) How do they differ: quantity by country, qualities, refining process? UN State of World Environmental Crisis in Food and Fisheries\nUN State of Animal Genetics USDA Adoption of Genetic Engineered Crops ISAAA Adoption of Biotech Crops\n3) How agri-commodities are treated/processed, transported (barge, rail, truck, vessel)? USDA, Food Crisis and Trade, Food Security Portal\n4) Superintendence: GIPSA, FOSFA, GAFTA, ANEC\n5) Agricultural products for energy - linkage between agricultural commodities and the energy businesses USDA Biobased Products Projection, Carbon Credits', 'S417055', '#1149CB'),
('2022-S417056', 'Sustainability & Business Ethics & Human Rights', 'The course will first provide a rigorous yet accessible introduction to the economics of sustainability. Participants will be presented with different definitions of sustainability (weak or strong) and identify the conditions to achieve it. Of special interest, is the role of corporate social responsibility.  \r\nThe course will then present the academic discipline of business ethics and how it deals with a broad range of issues that present operational and reputational risks to individuals, corporations, and industries. Being able to handle ethical issues professionally is a success factor for your career and your company\'s performance. It is also important for the standing of the industry in which you are involved. This module of business ethics presents ethical theories as a tool to reflect on standard practices in the workplace and for specific social and environmental risks in business operations in the commodity trading industry. Case illustrations and guest speakers will focus on typical business and human rights challenges in the commodity trading industry.', 'S417056', '#E852FB'),
('2022-S417057', 'Trade Finance Banking Instruments', 'The flow of a typical trade finance transaction\nMethods of payment\nUCP 600\nTransport documents\nLetters of indemnity\nTransferable, back-to-back, revolving, \'green/red clause\' documentary credit\nDeed of assignment\nSuretyship guarantees (bonds / \'cautions\')\nBid & performance bonds\nPayment guarantees and stand-by letters of credit\n\n', 'S417057', '#AC8E2D'),
('2022-S417058', 'Master thesis / Mémoire de maîtrise', NULL, 'S417058', '#A56453'),
('2022-S417059', 'Traineeship / Stage', NULL, 'S417059', '#35882A'),
('2022-S417060', 'Options', 'The course opens with the introduction to basic financial options, their payoffs and combinations of payoffs. The role of options in market completion is analyzed.\nFurther on the evaluation topic is introduced with theoretical closed-form models and binomial trees. This paves the way to the main Greeks, their understanding, utilizations and limitations.\nThe course closes with a brief discussion of Exotic Options and Real Options analysis.', 'S417060', '#5B92B3'),
('2022-S417070', 'Sustainable Economic Development', 'This course will introduce the economics of sustainable development. We will present and discuss the economic conditions to achieve sustainability and appraise the availability of natural resources for and the environmental consequences of sustained economic growth. The course will also provide an analysis of the measurement of mechanisms that relate population growth to the environment, the ecological footprint, the empirical evidence on the \'resource curse\' hypothesis and the role of biodiversity in supporting important ecosystems services and resilience.', 'S417070', '#1A6015'),
('2022-S417158', 'Energy II (Renewable)', 'The course covers essential technical characteristics of these commodities, quality differentiation and requirements, price discovery and benchmarks, and various contracts for physical or financial delivery. For electricity, it covers issues of grid stability and balancing. It takes place over two successive week-ends (Friday afternoon and Saturday morning, for a total of 12 hours per week). It features several outside speakers in addition to lectures delivered by the two professors in charge. It is evaluated on the basis of a take-home exam consisting in writing a short essay to address one of several questions proposed.\nThe course is open only to students enrolled in the Master of Science in Commodity Trading.', 'S417158', '#22F827'),
('2022-S421001', 'Quantitative methods', 'List of topics:\r\nIntroduction: why do we use quantitative methods?\r\nIdentification, inference and estimation\r\nWhat to estimate? The notion of causality\r\nUsing regressions to estimate causal effects\r\nApproaches to policy evaluation:\r\nRandomized experiments\r\nInstrumental variables\r\nDifferences-in-Differences\r\n', 'S421001', '#7DC716'),
('2022-S421005', 'Survival Analysis', 'Survival analysis is concerned with the analysis of responses which are times to an event of interest. Such events can be, for example, recurrence of disease, cessation of smoking, divorce, or weaning of breastfed infants. We seek to relate the time-to-event to covariates. In principle, we utilize the same tools that are applicable to other statistical analyses, such as multiple regression, but we have to take account of two issues that are common to time-to-event responses, viz. non-normality and censoring.\r\n\r\nThe models that we study accommodate these features, and enable us to assess the effect of covariates in the usual statistical framework of estimation and hypothesis testing. We will be using the statistical software R throughout the unit.\r\n\r\nThis course will mainly focus on the two main methods of analyzing time to event data, namely the Kaplan-Meier estimates and the Cox-proportional hazard model. With the help of examples we will go through each of these methods with a special emphasis on model building, model checking and finally results interpretation.', 'S421005', '#E360B8'),
('2022-S421010', 'Longitudinal Data Analysis', 'Apart from the essential theoretical knowledges required to understand the models, focus will be given on applied statistics, and the course will use two or three data set to illustrate all the concepts presented through the different courses. \nWe will first start with an introduction to longitudinal data; illustrated with several examples, and graphical representation. The basic ANOVA approach will be then presented, as well as its major limitations. Linear model for longitudinal data will be introduced, with the notions of correlation for the errors, maximum likelihood estimation, and restricted maximum likelihood estimation. \nThe concept of random effect will be then presented in the following courses, with focuses on the models for covariance structure, the inference about the parameters, and the comparison of different models.\nThe specific situation of binary data will also be presented.\nFinally we will address the practical issues of missing values, missing value mechanism, and sample size estimation.', 'S421010', '#8244FC'),
('2022-S601019', 'International Finance', 'This is a doctoral level course introducing students to important topics in international finance. The first part analyzes the risk-sharing properties of international business cycles, the classic literature (and recent advances) on international asset pricing, and issues of international asset allocation. The second part focuses on aspects of the recent financial crisis. Here we cover the role of asset market contagions, regulatory arbitrage and failure, current account imbalances and their sustainability, the European financial crisis and stability issues of a currency union.', 'S601019', '#EAF1E6'),
('2022-S601020', 'Empirical Research Design: From Research Question to Publication', 'This research course focuses on empirical research design and is particularly suitable for PhD students in the process of designing their empirical research projects. It is not a course on specific research methodologies but aims to give guidance on the main questions and decisions researchers face when developing research designs for empirical (quantitative or qualitative) projects. We will discuss the research process from the development of the research question to publication. The focus will be on three key themes. First, how to start your research and what to consider when formulating research questions. Second, what are the key decisions in choosing a research design and working with data. Third, what implications do these decisions have for the publication process. In particular, what are common questions referring to empirical research designs in the review process at top international journals.', 'S601020', '#8F2D59'),
('2022-S601021', 'Introduction Into Data Analysis Using R', 'The class will be taught in three daylong sessions. In the first session, we will cover fundamental concepts such as basic R programming skills, different types of variables, indexing, using packages, and calls to functions. In the second session, we will look into different ways of summarizing and plotting data descriptively and we will work with some frequently used statistical tests. The third day will be about more advanced graphical functions, statistical tests, and programming concepts. Students will be required to solve homework exercises after each session. \nStarting from day one, we will work with hands-on exercises and real-world data in front of the computer. Therefore, students are requested to bring their own laptop into class.\n', 'S601021', '#015ED0'),
('2022-S601025', 'Paper Writing and Publishing', 'This research course aims to share insights about how to write and publish a research paper in top academic journals. The course focuses on the main sections of a research paper, i.e., the introduction, theory background, theory development, methodology, findings, and discussion. When focusing on paper writing and publishing, we work on selected current debates in management (i.e., on organizational ambidexterity and Artificial Intelligence in management), rather than providing students with a complete overview of the literature. The students are expected to participate actively in group discussions and independently immerse themselves into the selected research streams.', 'S601025', '#A597DF'),
('2022-S601029', 'Reading Group in Economics and Econometrics', 'PhD students, post-docs and faculty members will read, present and discuss economics and econometrics papers that have been recently published in prestigious journals. The reading group will serve as a focal meeting place for PhD students and faculty in economics and econometrics. PhD students in all years, post-docs and faculty members are encouraged to participate in the reading group.', 'S601029', '#320048'),
('2022-S601032', 'Modern Flexible Regression', 'This course will introduce approaches to regression modelling for a large variety of continuous and discrete response variables. The cases of independent as well as correlated responses will be addressed.\n\nThe first part of the course will be devoted to univariate nonparametric regression, mostly via splines, even though we will briefly mention other alternatives.\n\nThe second part of the course will deal with generalized models for independent responses, in their linear parametric form (GLM) and in their nonparametric additive form (GAM). We will consider several settings within the exponential family of distributions (e.g. binomial, Poisson, Gamma,\') and beyond (negative binomial, beta,...).\n\nFinally, the third part of the course will consider extensions of these models. To model non independent responses, we will look at GEE, GLMM and GAMM models. To extend the flexibility of the structural relationships, GAMLSS will come at rescue.\n\nMany real data applications will illustrate the use of the models introduced and will be analyzed during the exercises sessions (labs), which are part of the course.\n\nThe R software will be used throughout.', 'S601032', '#1D7DB4'),
('2022-S601034', 'Financial Econometrics', NULL, 'S601034', '#6BE699'),
('2022-S601040', 'PhD Research Workshop', 'This is a one day workshop with presentations of PhD students on their respective research topics. There will be feedback on the presentations of the other PhD students and time for scientific discussions.', 'S601040', '#D5F33F'),
('2022-S601045', 'CUSO Doctoral School in Statistics and Applied Probability', 'The Doctoral School consists of lectures of 3 invited speakers who present different topics of statistics and applied probability. It takes place over 3 days and there is plenty of time to discuss and exchange with the speakers and among the PhD students.', 'S601045', '#01866E'),
('2022-S601046', 'CUSO Doctoral School in Statistics and Applied Probability', 'The Doctoral School consists of lectures of 3 invited speakers who present different topics of statistics and applied probability. It takes place over 3 days and there is plenty of time to discuss and exchange with the speakers and among the PhD students.', 'S601046', '#7D71FC'),
('2022-S999999', 'Cours Hors Faculté 3 ou 6 ou 9 crédits (maximum 9 crédits)', 'Hors faculté: Dans le cadre des Cours libres transversaux, l\'étudiant peut choisir des cours de bachelor en dehors de la GSEM, à l\'Université de Genève, pour un maximum de 9 crédits et n\'apparaissant pas dans la liste des cours interdits figurant dans les directives d\'applications du Bachelor en économie et management', 'S999999', '#4698AA'),
